 precision residual (n at or below the fixed point of its first positive-clock prefix): 0 members: []
== (3) the 2-adic distance to K and the record function ==
 dist_2(n, K) = 2^(-floor(tau(n) log_2 3)) (Lemma K1; proof in the note): (n, tau, exponent) for small n: [(1, 1, 1), (3, 2, 3), (5, 1, 1), (7, 4, 6), (9, 1, 1), (11, 3, 4), (15, 4, 6), (27, 37, 58)]
 records of tau(n) (first word-descent index) for odd n < 2 * 10^6: [(3, 2, 3), (7, 4, 6), (27, 37, 58), (703, 51, 80), (10087, 66, 104), (35655, 85, 134), (270271, 103, 163), (362343, 104, 164), (381727, 109, 172), (626331, 111, 175), (1027431, 115, 182), (1126015, 141, 223)]
 empirical Liouville exponent: dist_2(n, K) >= n^(-kappa) with kappa = max floor(tau log_2 3)/log_2 n over records = 12.198; the ratio tau/log_2 n at the last records: [5.63, 5.88, 5.76, 5.76, 7.01]
 f(N) = least n with floor(tau log_2 3) >= N grows like 2^(N/kappa) = 2^(0.08 N): the size price of the never-descending set, in the record-holders
